---
/**
 * The privacy page, written BEFORE any analytics exists rather than after.
 *
 * The product's promise is "no analytics", and that is a promise about the
 * application. A website is a different thing, and the moment a site with that
 * claim on its home page adds a counter without saying so, the claim stops
 * being worth anything — including the part that was true. So the distinction
 * gets drawn first, in public, and whatever is added later has to fit what is
 * written here.
 */
import Reading from '../layouts/Reading.astro';

const UPDATED = '2026-08-05';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'Privacy',
  url: 'https://topics.armonia.io/privacy',
  description: 'What the Topics application collects (nothing) and what this website collects, kept as two separate answers because they are two separate things.',
  dateModified: UPDATED,
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Armonia', url: 'https://armonia.io' },
  inLanguage: 'en',
};
---

<Reading
  title="Privacy · Topics"
  description="The Topics app collects nothing and talks to no server of ours. This website is a separate question, and this page answers both, including what would change if analytics were ever added."
  path="/privacy"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  current="docs"
>
  <header class="article-head">
    <p class="article-kicker">Updated {UPDATED}</p>
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="article-standfirst">
      Two questions that get mixed up constantly: what the application does, and what this
      website does. They have different answers and both are below.
    </p>
    <div class="article-meta"><time datetime={UPDATED}>{UPDATED}</time></div>
  </header>

  <div class="article-body">
    <article class="prose">
      <h2 id="the-application">The application</h2>
      <p>
        Topics collects nothing about how you use it: no analytics library, no crash
        reporter, no usage ping, no account. Your conversations, projects, boards and
        terminal history are SQLite files and directories under <code>~/.topics</code> on
        your own disk.
      </p>
      <p>
        One exception, and it is opt-in: reaching Topics from outside your own network is a
        paid feature that routes traffic through a relay. Use ours and that traffic passes
        through our infrastructure; point <code>TOPICS_RELAY_URL</code> at your own and it
        does not. With the relay off, nothing of yours leaves your machine.
      </p>
      <p>
        It does make network requests, and all of them go somewhere you chose: to Anthropic or
        OpenAI when an agent runs, with your key or through the CLI you are signed in to; to
        GitHub when it checks for an update; and to whatever a browser pane is pointed at. We
        never see any of it, because none of it comes to us.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is checkable rather than promised. The source is MIT on GitHub, and the
        network calls are the sort of thing a proxy or Little Snitch will tell you about in a
        minute flat. Do that instead of believing this paragraph.
      </p>

      <h2 id="this-website">This website</h2>
      <p>
        Static pages on Cloudflare, with <strong>no analytics, no cookies and no third-party
        scripts</strong> as of {UPDATED}. Nothing on the page profiles you, and there is no
        consent banner because there is nothing to consent to.
      </p>
      <p>Two things do happen, and they are worth naming:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare logs requests.</strong> Any web server does: IP address, user
          agent, which page. It is what serving a request consists of. We do not build
          anything on top of those logs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Three fonts and one API.</strong> Type comes from Fontshare and jsDelivr, and
          the download buttons ask <code>api.github.com</code> for the latest release. Those
          requests reach those companies, with your IP, exactly as any other request to them
          would.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The interactive demo is the real client running in an iframe with sample data, in your
        browser. It talks to no backend and stores nothing outside that tab.
      </p>

      <h2 id="if-analytics-are-ever-added">If analytics are ever added</h2>
      <p>
        They might be. Knowing whether anybody reads the blog is a reasonable thing to want.
        If it happens it will be Cloudflare Web Analytics, which is cookieless and records no
        cross-site identifier, and this page will say so with the date, before the script goes
        up rather than after.
      </p>
      <p>
        What will not happen: cookies, a third-party tag manager, any product that follows a
        person between sites, and anything at all inside the application. The promise on the
        home page is about the app, and adding a counter to a marketing page is not a reason
        to weaken it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="mailto:topics@armonia.io">topics@armonia.io</a>. If you find a network call
        the app makes that is not described here, that is a bug and we would like to know
        about it, and the <a href="https://github.com/armonia/topics-app/blob/main/SECURITY.md" rel="noopener" target="_blank">security policy</a> is the fast route.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Reading>
